dialect grammar
grammar stdio.svg.snapshot
extension svg
start document
comment none
string double raw
string single raw

# P2-FG3: real SVG 1.1 grammar of `stdio.svg`'s wire text -- `SvgSnapshot`'s persisted `doc` field
# IS a generic `XmlDocument` (`../../🦀️component.rs:17`) and its own `print_dsl`/`parse_dsl`
# (`../../🦀️component.rs`'s `write_svg_xml`/`parse_svg_xml`) route straight through
# `xml_document_to_text`/`xml_document_from_text` -- so the wire text this grammar recognizes is
# byte-for-byte the SAME shape `📰xml`'s own snapshot grammar recognizes (element/attribute/
# content-node recursion), restated here rather than `use`d (cross-artifact `use` doesn't resolve
# at walk/recognize time yet, `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §2.1) -- matching `xml`'s own just-landed FG1
# grammar's structural shape production-for-production, per this wave's brief. Only the
# `artifact-mark` header token and the `extension`/`grammar` ids differ.
#
# `comment none`: SVG (being XML) has no `#`-comment syntax, and numeric character references
# (`&#65;`, `&#x1F600;`) use `#` as a literal sigil immediately after `&` -- the shared lexer's
# default `#`-to-EOL comment would otherwise swallow the rest of the line at every such reference
# (the same STEP/DXF `#`-collision `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §1.1 documents, inherited verbatim from
# xml's own grammar).
#
# `string double raw` + `string single raw` (P2-M1 item 1): SVG attribute values are quoted with
# EITHER `"`/`'` (`parse_attr_value`, reused unchanged from xml) and are captured VERBATIM -- entity
# decoding only ever runs over element CONTENT text, never attribute values -- so `raw` is the
# honest mode for both delimiters.
#
# `<`/`>`/`&`/`;` below are the P2-M1-promoted single-char terminals (LT/GT/AMP/SEMICOLON); every
# other punctuation (`/` `:` `=` `[` `]` `!` `?` `#` `--`) was ALREADY a real lexer-alphabet
# single-char token (or, for `!`/`?`/`#`, a real text-addressable `Error` token once `comment none`
# stops `#` being swallowed).
artifact-mark = "stdio.svg"
document = artifact-mark declaration? doctype? element

# `<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>`.
declaration = LT "?" "xml" "version" "=" TEXT decl-encoding? decl-standalone? "?" GT
decl-encoding = "encoding" "=" TEXT
decl-standalone = "standalone" "=" TEXT

# `<!DOCTYPE name>` -- the demo/fixture's own real shape (a fuller external-ID/internal-subset form
# is preserved byte-for-byte Rust-side but out of THIS grammar's modeled scope, same
# opaque-unless-round-tripped-structurally treatment `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §6's PDF/DWG carve-out
# and xml's own sibling grammar use).
doctype = LT "!" "DOCTYPE" name GT

# `<name attr="v" ...>content</name>` / `<name attr="v" .../>`.
element = empty-elem-tag | s-tag content e-tag
empty-elem-tag = LT name attribute* "/" GT
s-tag = LT name attribute* GT
e-tag = LT "/" name GT
attribute = name "=" TEXT

# `:` as a name-start/name char (namespace prefixes, e.g. `xmlns:xlink`/`xlink:href` -- genuinely
# common on real SVG documents). The qualified alternative comes first for the same no-backtrack
# reason xml's own `name` production documents.
name = IDENT ":" IDENT | IDENT

# `(element | reference | cdSect | pi | comment | charData)*`. Every alternative starts on a
# mutually exclusive leading token, so alternative order here is inert.
content = {element | reference | cdata | xml-comment | pi | text-run}*

# Character data between tags, entity-decoded on read -- modeled as a run of plain word/number
# tokens, matching the demo fixture exactly; a fully general "any byte up to the next `<`/`&`"
# primitive doesn't exist at this token-based dialect layer (same consolidated gap xml's own
# grammar files, `text-run`/`cdata-body`/`comment-body`).
text-run = {IDENT | INT | FLOAT}+

# `&amp; &lt; &gt; &quot; &apos;` / `&#NNN;` / `&#xHHHH;`.
reference = entity-ref | char-ref
entity-ref = AMP IDENT SEMICOLON
char-ref = AMP "#" INT SEMICOLON | AMP "#" IDENT SEMICOLON

# `<![CDATA[...]]>` -- common inside real SVG `<style>`/`<script>` elements.
cdata = LT "!" "[" "CDATA" "[" cdata-body "]" "]" GT
cdata-body = {IDENT | INT | FLOAT}*

# `<!--...-->`. Named `xml-comment`, NOT the bare `comment` this construct's own real name would
# suggest -- `comment` collides with the five RESERVED header-directive keywords (`extension`/
# `use`/`start`/`comment`/`string`, `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §3 pitfall 3).
xml-comment = LT "!" "--" comment-body "--" GT
comment-body = {IDENT | INT | FLOAT}*

# `<?target data?>` processing instruction (any target except a bare `xml` is an ordinary PI).
pi = LT "?" name pi-data? "?" GT
pi-data = {IDENT | INT | FLOAT}+

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SVG-specific attribute-VALUE micro-grammars (per this wave's brief) -- documentary productions,
# deliberately UNREACHABLE from `start=document`. `attribute = name "=" TEXT` above already
# captures a whole quoted attribute value as ONE opaque `TEXT` token (M1's quote-delimited string
# mode swallows everything between the delimiters uninterpreted) -- there is no dialect-level
# mechanism to re-tokenize an already-consumed `TEXT` token's contents against a DIFFERENT,
# attribute-NAME-conditioned production (no "if the previous attribute's name was X, switch
# grammars for its value" primitive exists), so nothing below can ever be wired into the
# `document` production tree above. They exist purely to document, as precisely as the token
# dialect allows, the REAL internal structure this artifact's own Rust codec
# (`../../🦀️component.rs`'s `NumCursor`/`parse_view_box`/`parse_points`/`parse_transform_list`/
# `parse_path_data`) imposes once an attribute value string has been extracted -- matching the
# W0 census's own conclusion for this row ("inner mini-languages ... stay hand-written
# regardless") while still modeling everything that genuinely IS structural, per the brief.
#
# `viewBox="min-x min-y width height"` (`parse_view_box`) -- 4 comma-or-space-separated numbers
# (SVG's own `<comma-wsp>` grammar -- `NumCursor::skip_wsp_comma` treats a literal `,` and
# whitespace interchangeably as a separator, so the comma below is optional either way).
view-box-value = number ","? number ","? number ","? number
number = INT | FLOAT

# `points="x1,y1 x2,y2 ..."` (`parse_points`, polyline/polygon) -- an even-length run of
# comma/space-separated number pairs.
points-value = point {","? point}*
point = number ","? number

# `transform="translate(10,20) scale(2) rotate(90,5,5) ..."` (`parse_transform_list`) -- a
# space-separated list of `name(args)` function calls; `(`/`)` are already real single-char lexer
# tokens (distinct from this dialect's own `{ }` GROUPING metasyntax, `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §3
# pitfall 1), so this is real structure, not a fabricated approximation.
transform-value = transform-fn*
transform-fn = IDENT "(" number-list ")"
number-list = {number ","?}*

# `d="M10,10 L20,20 Z"` (`parse_path_data`) -- MODELED ONLY for the case where every command has
# its own EXPLICIT leading letter, matching `parse_path_data`'s own per-letter match arms and each
# command's fixed, letter-determined argument arity (SVG 1.1 spec §8.3.2). Two real behaviors of
# the Rust parser are genuinely NOT representable at this token-based layer -- filed as
# `mechanism_gaps` id `svg-path-data-implicit-and-flag-squeeze` (`engine_area`: grammar dialect /
# lexer): (1) IMPLICIT command repetition (a number run with no leading letter reuses the
# PREVIOUS command's letter -- a STATEFUL, not context-free, dispatch no production-based grammar
# can express); (2) the arc command's flag-digit SQUEEZE (`"A5 5 0 108 8"`'s `108` must decompose
# into flags `1`,`0` then the number `8`, per `NumCursor::parse_flag`'s own doc comment) -- but
# `108` has ALREADY been consumed as one atomic `INT` token by the shared lexer's tokenization
# pass, before any grammar-level production ever runs; no amount of grammar authoring can
# retroactively un-lex a token. Both gaps are pre-existing lexer/dialect limits, not bugs in this
# artifact's own Rust parser (which handles both correctly, per `path_arc_flag_squeeze_decomposes_correctly`
# in `../../🦀️component.rs`'s own test module).
d-value = path-command+
path-command = "M" number number | "L" number number | "H" number | "V" number | "C" number number number number number number | "S" number number number number | "Q" number number number number | "T" number number | "A" number number number flag flag number number | "Z"
flag = "0" | "1"

# `style="fill: red; stroke: blue"` (`parse_style_decls`) -- an honest opaque leaf, NOT modeled:
# the real Rust codec is a naive `;`-split/first-`:`-split KEY/VALUE pair extractor whose VALUE
# side (`red`, `#ff0000`, `2px`, `url(#g1)`, ...) is a plain, never-further-typed `String` -- there
# is no fixed grammar to restate here without fabricating structure the codec itself doesn't
# impose (`style`'s whole attribute value stays the opaque `TEXT` token `attribute` already
# captures above).
